x86_64-linux  One group, and it is not a mangling difference: the cxx1-only `C1`, `C2` and `D1` names are forms of constructors and destructors clang had no use for. With a virtual base C1 and C2 are genuinely different functions, and a class this case only ever builds as a base subobject needs no C1, one it only ever builds complete needs no C2, where cxx1 emits both for every constructor. `_ZTV1C` is the same shape one table over - C's vtable, emitted here and unwanted there. The VTTs and construction vtables agree since 2026-09-15.
arm64-darwin  The same group with Mach-O's leading underscore. Not the inline-definition trap the other cases record - this target emits both forms whatever the definition's place - but the *virtual base* half, where C1 and C2 differ and clang emits only the form it calls.
x86_64-windows  Two names cxx1 emits and clang does not, both of them definitions nothing here calls: `??0Two@@QEAA@XZ`, a constructor for a class this case only ever builds as a base subobject, and `??_DC@@QEAAXXZ`, C's vbase destructor - Itanium's D1 under its Microsoft name - where no complete C is ever destroyed. Emitted beside their classes rather than on demand, which is what the .nonames for x86_64-linux says about the C1 and D1 names one column over. Every name this case is about agrees, and names-vs-cl on the box checks them against cl rather than against clang's Microsoft emulation.
